# LaTeX build artefacts
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.lof
*.lot
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.bbl
*.blg
*.nav
*.snm
*.vrb
*.xmpi

# tagging / MathML sidecars
*-mathml.html
*-luamml-mathml.html
*.html

# test scratch
tests/cases/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# rendered baseline of nominal-capture.sh (regenerable, and large)
tests/.nominal-baseline/

# Emacs (AUCTeX / undo-tree)
auto/
*.~undo-tree~
*~
.#*

# built example/test PDFs are regenerable; keep only the manual
examples/*.pdf
tests/*.pdf
!linguexx-doc.pdf

# the linguex/linguexx timing benchmark: generated documents, their build
# artefacts and the raw timings, none of it reproducible across machines and
# none of it part of the package.  Everything in it regenerates from
# benchmarks/generate.py, run.py and summarize.py, which are ignored with it
# -- kept locally, deliberately, so the repository does not carry numbers
# that are true of one laptop.
benchmarks/

# implementation scaffolding -- plans and work orders addressed to whoever
# executes them.  Kept locally, not part of the distribution.  Note the
# pattern: a plan file put in doc/ will not be picked up by "git add" until
# this line is narrowed.
doc/*-PLAN.md

# the brace-look comparison: the renderings and timings behind the v1.2
# brace (tikz decoration against pict2e, hairline against a typographic
# pen, the tapered outline against Computer Modern's own \left\{).  What
# it concluded is in the CHANGELOG and in the comments of
# \__lxp_brace:nnn, where it belongs; the PDFs and the milliseconds are
# true of one laptop on one afternoon, like benchmarks/ above, and the
# patched copy of linguexx.sty in it is a copy nobody should ever load.
experiments/brace-look/
